  "names": {
    "order": "given family",
    "conventions": "Most people carry a first name and a family name, and the family name usually tells you the region or community: Sharma or Gupta in the north, Patil or Kulkarni in Maharashtra, Shah or Patel in Gujarat, Banerjee or Das in Bengal, Gowda or Shetty in Karnataka, Reddy or Rao in Telugu country, Nair or Menon in Kerala. Tamils often have no family name at all: the father's first name becomes the surname, or is written as an initial before the given name (R. Karthik, son of Ramasamy). Telugu people traditionally put the house name first (Kotha Srinivas), though offices often flip it. Sikh men carry Singh and Sikh women Kaur, often followed by a family name (Harpreet Singh Gill, Simran Kaur Sandhu). Muslim names are Arabic, Persian or Urdu (Mohammed Imran Khan). Goan, Mangalorean and East Indian Catholics have Portuguese family names (D'Souza, Fernandes). Strangers are addressed by role or kinship words (bhaiya, didi, anna, akka, uncle, aunty) rather than by name, often with the respectful ji.",
